---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import { lessons } from "../lib/course";
---

<Layout title="Make sense of trees">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">A FIELD GUIDE TO BINARY SEARCH TREES</p>
      <h1>
        Good things
        <br />
        grow in <em>balance.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="intro">
        A tree can make searching feel effortless. Or turn it into a very long
        walk. Let’s find out why—and learn how a few small moves change
        everything.
      </p>
      <a class="primary" href="/lessons/01-trees/">
        Begin the journey <span>↗</span>
      </a>
      <p class="micro">8 lessons · Interactive by design · Python included</p>
    </div>
    <div
      class="hero-art"
      aria-label="A balanced tree with root 40, children 20 and 60, and leaves 10, 30, 50, 70"
    >
      <span class="art-label">SAME VALUES. A BETTER SHAPE.</span>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 460 370" role="img">
        <title>A balanced seven-node binary search tree</title>
        <g class="hero-links">
          <path d="M230 65L125 170 M230 65L335 170 M125 170L65 275 M125 170L185 275 M335 170L275 275 M335 170L395 275" />
        </g>
        {[
          [230, 65, 40],
          [125, 170, 20],
          [335, 170, 60],
          [65, 275, 10],
          [185, 275, 30],
          [275, 275, 50],
          [395, 275, 70],
        ].map(([x, y, k], i) => (
          <g class:list={["hero-node", { accent: i === 0 }]}>
            <circle cx={x} cy={y} r={i === 0 ? 32 : 27} />
            <text x={x} y={y + 7} text-anchor="middle">
              {k}
            </text>
          </g>
        ))}
      </svg>
      <span class="art-note">Seven values. Just three levels.</span>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="opening">
    <p class="eyebrow">FIRST, AN INTUITION</p>
    <h2>
      It’s not just what you store.
      <br />
      It’s how you arrange it.
    </h2>
    <p>
      Imagine looking for a name in a stack of unsorted cards. You might read
      every card. Give those cards a little structure, and each decision can
      rule out a whole group. A search tree makes that idea tangible.
    </p>
    <p>
      We’ll start with paths and relationships, watch an ordinary tree lose its
      shape, then discover how AVL trees keep those paths short. The code comes
      after the idea clicks.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="course" class="course">
    <div class="section-head">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">THE COURSE</p>
        <h2>
          From first branch
          <br />
          to balanced tree.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        Read in order. Pause to experiment.
        <br />
        Every lesson builds on the last.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="lesson-list">
      {lessons.map((l, i) => (
        <a href={`/lessons/${l.slug}/`}>
          <span class="lesson-number">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>
          <div>
            <h3>{l.title}</h3>
            <p>{l.subtitle}</p>
          </div>
          <span class="duration">{l.minutes} min</span>
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="preview">
    <p class="eyebrow">A LITTLE PREVIEW</p>
    <h2>Watch order become imbalance.</h2>
    <p>
      Give both trees 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70. One keeps growing sideways.
      The other makes room.
    </p>
    <Demo kind="compare" />
    <a class="text-link" href="/lessons/07-unwind/">
      Understand what’s happening →
    </a>
  </section>
</Layout>
